\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The discriminant wall $\Sigma = 0$ of the announced map in the chart $C = 1$ with coordinates $A' = AC^2$ and $B' = BC$, which preserves the wall and the regions because $\Sigma (AC^2, BC, 1) = C^2\,\Sigma (A,B,C)$. Left: the whole range of the sampled targets; right: the neighborhood of the cusp at $(0.1481, 1.3333)$. The shaded region ($D > 0$) has three real preimages; outside it there is one. Triangles are sampled targets with three real preimages, squares with one, the star is the escape target $(0,1,1)$, and the cross at the cusp is the point of $\Gamma $ in this chart, which the image omits. The curves are drawn from the exact polynomial of \eqref  {eq:sigma}.}}{20}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:wall}{{4}{20}{The discriminant wall $\Sigma = 0$ of the announced map in the chart $C = 1$ with coordinates $A' = AC^2$ and $B' = BC$, which preserves the wall and the regions because $\Sigma (AC^2, BC, 1) = C^2\,\Sigma (A,B,C)$. Left: the whole range of the sampled targets; right: the neighborhood of the cusp at $(\numWallCuspA , \numWallCuspB )$. The shaded region ($D > 0$) has three real preimages; outside it there is one. Triangles are sampled targets with three real preimages, squares with one, the star is the escape target $(0,1,1)$, and the cross at the cusp is the point of $\Gamma $ in this chart, which the image omits. The curves are drawn from the exact polynomial of \eqref {eq:sigma}}{figure.caption.9}{}}
